Sotto — in-browser tutor, run 9: tool calls on Qwen3.5-2B / Qwen3.5-4B (WebLLM 0.2.84)
=======================================================================================

Task: TUTOR_TIER=large e2e (apps/client/e2e/browser-tutor.mjs) had the 4B answer
"Guardé **cigarra** para ti." with no tool block, so save_vocabulary never ran
(assertion 9). Goal: reliable tool calls on both Qwen3.5 builds.

All runs: KEEP_PROFILE=1, warm model caches, fake mic = Kokoro wav of
"¿Qué significa la palabra cigarra?" then "Por favor, guarda la palabra cigarra."
Logs of every run are summarized below; raw logs were kept in the session
scratchpad only.

Reproduction (unchanged code, large tier)
-----------------------------------------
repro-large: 9/9 PASS — but only because utterance 1 played while the models
were still loading, so the SAVE request was the session's FIRST LLM call. A
"Sorry, something went wrong there" caption appeared right after the save.
fix2-large (fresh build, both utterances heard): assertion 9 FAIL —
  llm_turn_ms=6769ms no-tool raw="...He guardado **cigarra** en tu vocabulario..."
i.e. the reported failure, reproduced: turn 2 narrates the save.

Root causes found (four, all in packages/voice/src/browser-cascade)
-------------------------------------------------------------------
1. worker.ts WebLlmEngine.chat: the JSON-tool fallback instruction was only
   added inside the catch that discovers `tools` is rejected — i.e. on the
   ONE call that hit the throw. Every later turn of the session went out
   with no `tools` parameter and no instruction at all. This is the whole
   intermittency: the save worked whenever it was the first LLM call and
   narrated whenever it was the second. Fix: inject the instruction on
   every call once `supportsTools` is false.
2. WebLLM's fixed Qwen conversation template has no `tool` role: the
   continuation after a tool result threw `Role is not supported: tool`
   (llm_pipeline_failed -> the "Sorry, something went wrong" caption, on
   every run, right after a save that had succeeded). Fix (tool-protocol.ts):
   re-render the OpenAI-shaped history into Qwen's own shape for the
   fallback — tool results as a user turn wrapping <tool_response>, the
   assistant's call as its text plus the <tool_call> block.
3. Barge-in hang (standard tier, fix5-standard): after speech_start
   interrupted turn 1, turn 2 never produced an LLM call (session sat in
   `thinking` for 130s). WebLLM 0.2.84 asyncGenerate releases its per-model
   lock only when the generator runs to its end; `break`ing out of
   `for await` on abort ends the generator at the current yield, the lock
   is never released, and the next create() blocks forever on
   lock.acquire(). Fix: on abort keep draining (interruptGenerate() stops it
   within one decode step) instead of `break`.
4. Protocol shape: the instruction now carries Qwen's native
   `<tools>…</tools>` / `<tool_call>{json}</tool_call>` block (the text its
   chat template would render for native tools; WebLLM's non-Jinja template
   never injects it), and the parser accepts that, the legacy ```tool fence,
   ```json / bare fences, and a bare JSON object naming a known tool.
   markers.ts strips / holds back <tool_call> the same way it did ```tool.

Diagnostics added (local console only, never leave the browser)
---------------------------------------------------------------
- llm_turn_ms=<ms> tool=<names> | no-tool raw="<first 400 chars>"
- tool_result=<ms> <tool> <args> -> ok | error=<executor message>
- [sotto-tutor] error <code>: <message> (provider.ts, for recoverable errors)

